  "reason": "With no prior experiments, there is complete uncertainty about the performance landscape. The most informative first step is to establish a simple, well-understood baseline: RNA-only with the simplest encoder (PCA, 2000 components) and singleprofile (no aggregation or fusion decisions needed). This experiment tests the fundamental question of how much signal a basic RNA representation carries for the classification task. It serves as a reference floor against which all subsequent experiments—multiprofile aggregation, learned RNA encoders (scvi, scgpt), image-only, and multimodal fusion—can be meaningfully compared. Choosing the minimal configuration first avoids conflating multiple design variables and ensures that any future gains are attributable to specific architectural choices rather than an already-complex baseline."
